In March 2026, Karpathy&rsquo;s autoresearch1 put recursive self-improvement back in the spotlight. An agent edits a training script, runs it, and keeps the change if a metric improves. Since then, Shopify&rsquo;s CEO,2 Google Research3 and researchers4,5 alike have been running loops like it. In each, an automated score decides what the loop keeps.
If the eval is how we judge the loop, the loop is only as good as the eval. The problem is that an eval is a small part of what the system could be asked.
An eval is a slice #Picture every question a system could receive, and every answer it could give. That space is huge. An eval is a slice of it: a few hundred or thousand cases we thought of, wrote down and know how to score.
Eval A bigger eval Everything it could be asked A loop improves the system on that slice. Outside the slice, nothing pushes back. The score cannot see those cases, so a change that quietly breaks them looks the same as a change that does nothing.
We should still make the slice as big as we can: more cases, cases taken from real failures, generated edge cases. But even a big slice will never account for every edge case and situation a system meets.
The score on the slice is only a proxy #Inside the slice, the score is still a proxy, and a loop will optimize the proxy. Text metrics like ROUGE and BERTScore, retrieval metrics and a model used as a judge each reward something close to what we want, not the thing itself. A model judge can also be inconsistent on subjective cases, so it needs calibrating against human grades.6
Optimizing on this slice also has a cost we do not see. A change that raises the score can quietly wear down what the system does well everywhere else, so while we optimize the slice, we should watch that the system&rsquo;s general knowledge and capabilities do not regress. My last post has an example: rules added to avoid worst cases piled up and clashed with each other.
The person who used to steer is gone #Before AI coding, a human iterated by hand. They reviewed the results at each step and decided the direction, the methods and the approach, so they often noticed what the eval missed.
In a self-improving loop, the agent makes those calls itself. A model is not a human, and it does not make the same judgement calls. The direction it picks may not be the one a person would choose, or would accept. The score is what holds it in place, and the score covers only a slice.
Before Change Score Human reviews, then steers Now Change Score Agent sees only the score Human Yet that is not an argument against these loops. A loop can try far more ideas than a person iterating by hand. The autoresearch README estimates about 100 experiments while you sleep,1 which is why self-improvement speeds up system development so much. The aim is to keep that speed and add back some of the judgement the human used to supply.
What I do about it #Look at user feedback and try to understand their intent. It is the one signal that comes from outside the slice. A complaint tells you what they were trying to do, and every one becomes a permanent case, so the slice grows from what users actually hit.
I think much of system development can now be handed to agents and loops, and that will only grow. But not all of it. The users are the ones who know what the system is for, so it is not fully hands-off.
